The service stops responding after a handful of failed requests, even though the database is healthy. What is the bug?

```js
async function getUser(id) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const { rows } = await client.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  client.release();
  return rows[0];
}
```

Under load, this transfer sometimes commits one update without the other. Why?

```js
await pool.query('BEGIN');
await pool.query('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - $1 WHERE id = $2', [amt, from]);
await pool.query('UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + $1 WHERE id = $2', [amt, to]);
await pool.query('COMMIT');
```

A page lists 100 users with their latest order. The endpoint is slow, and the database log shows 101 queries per request. What is the standard fix?

```js
const users = await userRepo.findMany({ take: 100 });
for (const u of users) {
  u.latestOrder = await orderRepo.findLatestFor(u.id);
}
```

Startup validation rejects a correct environment where `PORT=3000`. Why, and what is the usual fix?

```js
import { z } from 'zod';
const Env = z.object({ PORT: z.number().int(), DATABASE_URL: z.string().url() });
export const env = Env.parse(process.env);
```

Errors from route handlers never reach this handler; Express sends its default HTML error page instead. Why?

```js
app.use((err, req, res) => {
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'internal' });
});
```

An AWS Lambda function talks to Postgres. During a traffic spike the database refuses connections with "too many clients". What is the main problem, and the right fix?

```js
export const handler = async (event) => {
  const pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL, max: 10 });
  const { rows } = await pool.query('SELECT …');
  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(rows) };
};
```

During deploys, some in-flight requests fail with "Cannot use a pool after calling end on the pool". What is wrong with this shutdown code?

```js
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => {
  await pool.end();
  server.close(() => process.exit(0));
});
```

A BullMQ worker charges a card and then updates the order. A worker crashed right after the charge succeeded, BullMQ retried the job, and the customer was charged twice. What is the robust fix?

```js
new Worker('payments', async (job) => {
  await psp.charge(job.data.orderId, job.data.amount);
  await orders.markPaid(job.data.orderId);
});
```

In this Drizzle code, the stock decrement is sometimes kept even though creating the order failed and the transaction rolled back. Why?

```js
await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await db.update(products).set({ stock: sql`stock - 1` }).where(eq(products.id, pid));
  await tx.insert(orders).values({ productId: pid, userId });
});
```

Non-admins get a 403, but the logs also show "Cannot set headers after they are sent", and the admin action still runs for them. What is wrong?

```js
const requireRole = (role) => (req, res, next) => {
  if (req.user?.role !== role) {
    res.status(403).json({ error: 'forbidden' });
  }
  next();
};
app.delete('/users/:id', requireRole('admin'), deleteUser);
```

When a popular cache key expires, 300 concurrent requests in the same Node.js process all query the database. A teammate proposes this change. What does it achieve?

```js
const inflight = new Map();
async function getProduct(id) {
  const hit = await redis.get(`p:${id}`);
  if (hit) return JSON.parse(hit);
  if (!inflight.has(id)) {
    inflight.set(id, loadAndCache(id).finally(() => inflight.delete(id)));
  }
  return inflight.get(id);
}
```

Checkout latency explodes whenever the shipping-quote API is slow, and the pool reports all clients checked out. What is the main design flaw?

```js
await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  const order = await tx.insert(orders).values(input).returning();
  const quote = await shippingApi.quote(order[0]); // HTTP call, 0.2–8 s
  await tx.update(orders).set({ shipping: quote.price }).where(eq(orders.id, order[0].id));
});
```

Review this authentication middleware that uses the `jsonwebtoken` package. Which two problems let an attacker get in? (Choose two.)

```js
function auth(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  const payload = jwt.decode(token);
  if (!payload || payload.exp * 1000 < Date.now()) return res.sendStatus(401);
  req.user = { id: payload.sub, role: payload.role };
  next();
}
```

An idempotency-key middleware lets two simultaneous retries of the same `POST /payments` both charge the customer. How should the key be claimed?

```js
const existing = await db.select().from(idem).where(eq(idem.key, key));
if (existing.length) return res.status(existing[0].status).json(existing[0].body);
const result = await createPayment(req.body);
await db.insert(idem).values({ key, status: 201, body: result });
```
